CASE 01 — Greenhouse ATS Migration & Regional Systems Implementation

Context
During a global migration to Greenhouse, 4–6 regional SMEs were appointed to configure and implement the system locally. I served as the U.S. regional SME, responsible for system configuration, workflow design, template setup, automation sequencing, and training 60+ interviewers across the region during a two-month rollout window.

The Problem
Our legacy ATS contained deeply inconsistent data and workflows. There were approximately 40–50 overlapping source tags. Reporting was unreliable. Recruiters manually entered data inconsistently. Interview scorecards varied by role and manager. Candidate communications were fragmented and often manual.

The risk wasn’t just migration. The risk was institutionalizing the same inconsistency inside a new system.

Design & Approach
Before rollout, I mapped the inconsistencies in source tagging and recommended structured categories and subcategories. I advocated for automatic source capture wherever Greenhouse supported it to reduce manual entry variability. When central implementation leads underestimated system capabilities, I referenced Greenhouse documentation directly to clarify what could be configured and automated.

I redesigned interview scorecards to improve evaluation clarity and consistency. I built tokenized email templates with timed automation triggers to standardize candidate communications. I reduced manual intake steps between role approval and requisition creation to streamline recruiter workflow.

I trained and supported 60+ interviewers, monitored system adoption, and reinforced disciplined usage.

Impact
Manual data entry points were reduced. Source tracking became structured and usable for reporting. Interview scorecards became consistent across teams. Candidate communications became automated and brand-aligned. Recruiters spent less time correcting data and coordinating manually.

Why It Matters

Hiring operations depend on reliable infrastructure. Clean configuration, disciplined taxonomy, and automation reduce downstream friction and make forecasting, reporting, and planning trustworthy.

